00:00:06.232 --> 00:00:08.690
RYAN LIPPERT: Thanks a lot,
everyone, for joining us today.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:10.565
So I'm going to be joined
by Venky Iyer here.

00:00:10.565 --> 00:00:11.830
My name is Ryan Lippert.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.371
And what we'll be
talking about today

00:00:13.371 --> 00:00:14.830
is just the
advantage of managed.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.246
And when we talk
about that, we're

00:00:16.246 --> 00:00:20.390
really talking about Hadoop and
migrating that from AWS to GCP.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.130
Now, before I turn
things over to Venky,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.210
he's the real star
of the show here,

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.060
what I'm going to do is just
quickly walk through Dataproc

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.442
in general and what we
see as our advantages.

00:00:30.442 --> 00:00:32.650
But really, that's going to
be hit home a lot better,

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.660
I think, by Venky whenever
he gets up and tells

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.160
his own customer
story about things.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.899
So let's talk about Dataproc
and just what is it,

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:42.190
and then why do we think
that's important from a Google

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:43.216
perspective.

00:00:43.216 --> 00:00:45.090
So I think that the best
place to start there

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:45.970
if you're going to
talk about Dataproc

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.940
is to talk about traditional
Spark and Hadoop clusters,

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.130
and the way that we think
about those, and the way

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:54.220
that those actually go into
implementation and operation.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.370
So with the traditional cluster,
what you end up having is,

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.560
within your cluster, you
have your data technology,

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:00.580
your governance.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:01.870
You have security in there.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.390
And then that lies on top of
operations and management.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:06.640
Now, the issue with those
traditional Spark and Hadoop

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.590
clusters is that they're
not really integrated

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:09.887
with other tools.

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:11.470
They've got to be
shared between many.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.000
And it's really difficult
to make those changes.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.880
And those changes end
up creating something

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.970
that we like to call the
de-value chain of big data,

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:19.981
to some extent.

00:01:19.981 --> 00:01:21.730
And so this is probably
familiar to anyone

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.460
who's provisioned Hadoop
clusters on-premises one

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:25.450
way or the other.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.460
But you've got to
obtain servers first.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.070
From there, you have
to configure servers.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:33.010
After that, you're installing
the open source software.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.200
Then you're configuring
that software.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.120
From there, you're
optimizing it.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.472
Then you need to debug it.

00:01:38.472 --> 00:01:40.180
And then you can
finally get to the point

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.320
where you're ready to
start to process data.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.160
And so that's a long cycle.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.750
And anytime that you want to
start that cycle over again

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.910
to scale, you've got
to go through each one

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:50.710
of those individual steps.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.480
Likewise, if you get
to the end of things

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:53.938
and you say, hey,
actually, what we

00:01:53.938 --> 00:01:55.960
need to do is a
reconfiguration here,

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.760
you're looking at
restarting that halfway

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.090
through the process.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.480
And so what you end up
with is a lot of lost time.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:05.099
And then that lost time is
also sunk costs that end up

00:02:05.099 --> 00:02:07.390
going in, to getting to a
point where, really, what you

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.275
want to up doing is processing
data at the end of the day.

00:02:10.275 --> 00:02:12.400
And so if we can get closer
to that processing data

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.170
piece of things, that's
going to be really

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.820
what our customers are
actually looking for.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:20.320
So the cycle that we just
showed on that last slide,

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:21.500
it usually leads to pain.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.190
So the first kind of
pain is scaling pain.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:27.220
So anytime that you need
to add new capacity in,

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:28.840
you end up with a
lag between when

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.040
your needs come on and then
when you can actually get that

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.577
out and into production.

00:02:34.577 --> 00:02:36.910
And that's because there's
just so much time is required

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:38.743
to be able to actually
get the truck rollout

00:02:38.743 --> 00:02:39.700
and make that happen.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.967
There's also an issue from an
utilization pain perspective.

00:02:42.967 --> 00:02:45.550
So the way that you usually see
it is you've got your cluster.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:46.330
It's up and running.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.371
It's taking in data at
the time that it needs to.

00:02:48.371 --> 00:02:50.140
Its processing data
as it needs to.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.610
But then there are big
inactive times as well.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:54.790
And so you're still paying
for that inactive time,

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.342
particularly if it's not
a managed type solution.

00:02:57.342 --> 00:02:59.050
And then what that
leads to is cost pain.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.582
And in cost pain,
what you'd see is

00:03:00.582 --> 00:03:03.040
you're still reducing your
costs as you move to Hadoop over

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.135
legacy technologies.

00:03:04.135 --> 00:03:06.010
But what you're doing
is, because you've over

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.950
provisioned, and because
you've got inactive clusters,

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.444
your cost is always going to
be more than what it actually

00:03:11.444 --> 00:03:13.360
should be if you could
actually get to a point

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.834
where you're only
using what you need.

00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:17.500
And so that's why we
think that, really,

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.540
the cluster and the platform
should work together.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.310
And that's what we've
tried to build here.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.320
And so when we think
about that, the stuff

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.180
that you'd rather not
care about every time,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.520
that's the governance, the
security, the operations

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:28.240
and management.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:29.830
Those are all very
important things

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:31.624
when it comes to
running a cluster,

00:03:31.624 --> 00:03:33.290
but it's not really
what you care about.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.770
And that's the data technology
that you're running in it.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:36.910
You really want to
get to the point

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:38.050
where you're looking
at your analytics,

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.310
you're looking at the
solutions, and you're

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.140
looking at the data
processing, and being

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.660
able to focus on that
as opposed to everything

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.670
else within your stack.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.720
And so that's why we've
developed Cloud Dataproc.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.600
And that's where we think
that our solution's pretty

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.016
differentiated from what you'd
see out there in the market

00:03:52.016 --> 00:03:52.780
more broadly.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.930
So Cloud Dataproc is just--
it's fast, it's easy to use,

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.180
it's low cost,
it's fully managed,

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.122
and it's all on Google
Cloud Platform, which

00:04:00.122 --> 00:04:02.080
means that it's integrated
with the other tools

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.851
that we have within
our platform as well.

00:04:03.851 --> 00:04:05.350
And so that really
allows you to get

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.150
up and moving a lot
faster than you would

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.400
be able to if you were trying
to run this on your own,

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.220
whether in a cloud and
just doing your self-hosted

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.080
or on-premises.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.079
And so our vision,
as I mentioned, fast.

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:18.370
So let's make sure that it
takes seconds to spin things

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:22.300
up, not minutes, not weeks,
definitely not months,

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.830
as it can sometimes be.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.247
We want things to
be easy as well.

00:04:25.247 --> 00:04:26.871
So be an expert with
your data, but not

00:04:26.871 --> 00:04:28.300
necessarily the infrastructure.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.786
Let's focus on what we're
doing from a data perspective.

00:04:30.786 --> 00:04:32.410
And then let's make
it so that you only

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:36.185
pay for what you're using,
as opposed to everything

00:04:36.185 --> 00:04:38.560
that you've got static that
could be in your stack that's

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.641
not actually working on things.

00:04:40.641 --> 00:04:42.390
And so when we actually
look, particularly

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.770
at that last point from
a cost perspective,

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.560
we've had some analysts come
out with some studies that

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.460
say that when you look at Cloud
Dataproc, particularly relative

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:54.130
to on-premises, it's 57%
less expensive than that.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:55.990
And then even when
you look at EMR,

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:59.430
it's going to be
32% less expensive.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.160
So the way that that
ends up happening

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.160
is as we move from
on-premises to just doing it

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.280
on Compute Engine, or
potentially in another cloud,

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.530
then all the way across
to Cloud Dataproc, what

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.320
you see is that on-premises,
you're self-managed

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.440
for everything, from code down
to the creation and deployment.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.110
And then as you start to
move from left to right

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:21.770
on the screen, from a
Compute Engine perspective,

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:23.990
Google starts to take over
things like connectivity,

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.504
deployment, and creation.

00:05:25.504 --> 00:05:27.170
But when you get
towards Cloud Dataproc,

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:30.380
you can see that the only thing
that the customers and users

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.500
end up being responsible for
is really getting to a point

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.260
where they're putting
in their custom code.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.190
And then everything
else along the way

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.500
is taken care of for you.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.320
And so when we
generally look at folks

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:44.240
who are starting to do
these migrations one

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:47.300
way or the other, if it's a lift
and shift from another cloud,

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.220
if it's even a lift and
shift within Google,

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.830
from self-hosted to
running Dataproc,

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:55.170
or particularly if it's
on-prem to into the cloud,

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.860
there's a set of six
common principles

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.760
that we would typically
look at and say, hey,

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:01.620
here's what you should
be thinking about.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.680
The first is separating
compute and storage.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:05.810
As you move into Dataproc,
the ability to do that

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.660
is going to allow you to
move a lot more efficiently.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:10.420
Use ephemeral and
long-lived clusters.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:13.106
So start to try to classify
them in one way or the other.

00:06:13.106 --> 00:06:15.230
If you have a cluster that
always needs to stay on,

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:16.910
make sure that you've
got the data that

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:19.319
needs to be processed
in that cluster.

00:06:19.319 --> 00:06:21.860
And then the ones that you can
spin up and spin down quickly,

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:23.700
separate those out.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.670
You also want to right size the
clusters per use case as well.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:28.370
So understand what
your use case is

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.930
and spin up clusters that
makes sense for that.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:32.936
This create and
delete clusters often

00:06:32.936 --> 00:06:34.310
goes very well
with the ephemeral

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:35.700
and long-lived clusters.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:37.750
There's no need to keep
those up and running

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:39.500
if you can spin them down.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.380
And then with a 60 to
90 second spin up time

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:44.060
for those clusters,
you can bring them

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:45.980
back anytime that you need to.

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:48.590
Replacing HDFS
with Cloud Storage.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:51.620
And then finally, using
Dataproc jobs through the APIs

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.370
that we offer.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.430
So that's a good
Google overview,

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:57.140
but I think what
everyone's really here

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.420
for is to hear more from a
customer, to hear about how

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.000
this actually goes,
potentially warts

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.390
and all, whenever it looks
like a lift and shift here.

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.610
So with that, I'd like
to introduce Venky.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:09.980
Venky is a software
engineer from Thumbtack.

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:12.627
So everyone give a good
hand to Venky here.

00:07:12.627 --> 00:07:15.012
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:17.860
VENKY IYER: Thanks, Ryan.

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:18.951
Hello, everyone.

00:07:18.951 --> 00:07:19.450
I'm Venky.

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:22.000
I'm a software
engineer at Thumbtack.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.580
In the fall of 2016, we decided
to move our self-hosted Hadoop

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.700
setup, data infrastructure
setup, from AWS

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.720
to managed services
on Google Cloud.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.430
And today I'm here to
tell you that story.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:40.510
But before I begin, let
me offer you a quick intro

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:43.302
on what is Thumbtack.

00:07:43.302 --> 00:07:45.510
In the world around us, it
would seem that capitalism

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:48.360
exists to make people lazier.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.930
And we do our bit by trying
to solve this problem.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:55.710
Why is it so hard to hire
a plumber or a gardener

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.330
or a photographer?

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:00.270
You can buy anything
you want with one click.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:03.400
You can book travel around the
world in a matter of minutes.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.810
But when you want to hire
a piano tutor for your kid,

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.730
you are forced to
use the analog world.

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:10.170
Maybe talk to somebody.

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:13.050
Look up Yelp or Yellow Pages
and try and make a few calls

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.150
and see what works out.

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:17.520
Thumbtack is an
online marketplace

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.550
that connects customers
to professionals

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:23.760
who match their specific job
requirements, their schedule,

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.050
and their budget.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.210
And Thumbtack is
also a platform that

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.070
enables professionals
and small business

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:32.250
owners to focus on
what they do best,

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.159
to focus on their core trade.

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:38.039
And Thumbtack works on their
behalf to acquire customers

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:40.240
with high intent for them.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.539
And like any other
internet company,

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:46.000
internet business
in today's age,

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.900
we are highly reliant on data.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:52.320
We need data for
core broad features,

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.250
like matching that I just
talked about for paid customer

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.800
acquisition through
SEM channels.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.420
We need timely and reliable data
to inform our top line metrics,

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.060
like customer
growth and revenue.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.730
We need data for
analyzing our experiments.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:11.700
We need data to train our
machine learning models.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:15.460
I'm sure a lot of you will
find this all too familiar.

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:19.950
So let's begin this story of
how we at Thumbtack attempted

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:22.500
to solve for these requirements.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:29.290
And we begin with a
state we were in a little

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:30.780
more than three years ago--

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:35.000
the V0 infrastructure,
or rather the lack of it.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:37.640
So we had this PHP
website, and we

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:40.010
used to run a daemon
that would collect events

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:41.735
from those website
servers, which

00:09:41.735 --> 00:09:45.980
we would dump it into
MongoDB, for whatever reason.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:49.640
We had a bunch of Python scripts
that would read those events,

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:52.640
do some data aggregation,
and do an in-place update,

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:54.530
again, for whatever reason.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:57.960
And creating this
infrastructure was very slow.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:01.560
We could analyze only
one experiment at a time.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.190
And if, by chance, you
wanted to find insights

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:06.170
into relational
data, you kind of

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:09.180
go into a completely different
world of a looker instance,

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.890
which was hooked on
to a live PG replica.

00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:17.270
So very fragile,
very slow, not very

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:19.910
feature rich, and
yet somehow comprised

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:21.856
of complex and convoluted code.

00:10:21.856 --> 00:10:23.480
So this was clearly
a problem statement

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:26.190
that we had to attempt to solve.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.940
And that began with our first
generation of infrastructure.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:35.850
So we set up Cloudera Hadoop on
a bunch of AWS EC2 instances.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:37.650
We set up Spark on it.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.560
We converted all
our data processing

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.050
from like random Python
scripts, or whatever,

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:46.440
to Scala, essentially
Spark jobs.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:49.870
We wrote all the data
output in Parquet format.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:53.040
We configured Cloudera
impala as a SQL engine

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:55.140
on top of that Parquet data.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:00.210
And we used a visualization
tool called Mode and hooked it

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:03.540
on to the Cloudera Impala
so that our analysts can now

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:06.000
create fancy dashboards.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.660
This infrastructure was
the first generation

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:12.900
that powered our experiment
visualization tool

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:14.400
that we called Prospect.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:19.230
It also powered our first
ML Prediction API serving,

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:22.390
which we called Matchbox.

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:24.020
And we set up Airflow.

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:27.340
We used Airflow to
orchestrate all these jobs,

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:30.970
to define the
schedules, to define

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:34.420
the dependencies between jobs,
on which should run after what.

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:38.610
Overall, a huge step up
from where we were before,

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:41.310
but not without its problems.

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:45.180
The first of which was
we were continuously

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:48.780
playing musical chairs
with the capacity, right?

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:52.290
This is where the de-value
chain that Ryan talked

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:55.000
about kind of commences for us.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.120
Jobs would step on each other.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:00.420
Invariably, what would happen is
a lot of scheduled Mode reports

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:03.090
would be scheduled to
run at 7:00 AM or 8:00 AM

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:06.090
so that people get fresh data
when they come to the office.

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:08.850
And they would
overload the cluster.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:12.180
Very frequently, we would field
queries like, hey, my dashboard

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:13.250
looks off.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:15.700
Is it a data problem,
or did we really

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:17.820
tank our revenue yesterday?

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.830
So we clearly needed
to do something

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:22.860
about the reliability
of data there.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.190
And ironically, in
spite of all that,

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:29.070
during midday, if nobody's
running any ad hoc query's,

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:31.080
then data cluster was idle.

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:33.270
So there was very
little elasticity

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:36.120
in our infrastructure
that we had set up.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.860
Data was replicated
within the HDFS.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:41.430
It maintains multiple
copies, but that was it.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:44.450
So we had this slight
durability risk as well.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:47.550
And what it meant as
a team for us is this

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:54.390
was a huge time sink in terms
of operational workload.

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.670
For engineers from
Google and Facebook

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:59.274
and the like to appreciate
this a bit more,

00:12:59.274 --> 00:13:00.690
I would like to
tell that this was

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:03.660
a team of six engineers
responsible for everything,

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:06.000
from setting up the
clusters to setting up

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.930
Hadoop and Spark on top of it
to actually writing the data

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:12.262
infrastructure that
we really needed.

00:13:12.262 --> 00:13:13.470
So this was a huge time sink.

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:15.178
So we had to kind of--
we were at a point

00:13:15.178 --> 00:13:17.790
where we had to start
thinking forward on how do we

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:19.840
solve these problems.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:21.760
And these were our alternatives.

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:24.070
The most obvious one
was to double down

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:27.400
on the same path, which would
have inevitably translated

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:31.810
into separating out our
compute and analysis clusters.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:35.140
Which would have meant
investing in some cluster

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:39.220
management tools that would
help us with capacity planning.

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:42.520
Maybe it would automate
some operational tasks.

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:45.280
But to summarize what
this would have meant

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:49.206
is we would have had to hire
our way out of that thing.

00:13:49.206 --> 00:13:50.830
And for companies
like ours, that thing

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:53.450
is a lot easier said than done.

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:55.720
So that was when
we started thinking

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:58.060
about managed solutions.

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:02.160
And these are the
two options that we

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:03.760
got to compare head on head.

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:07.330
So Dataproc and BigQuery
offerings on Google,

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:11.470
and EMR and RedShift
offering on AWS.

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:15.170
So comparing Dataproc
and EMR, EMR at the time

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:17.566
took about 30 minutes
to spin up a cluster.

00:14:17.566 --> 00:14:18.940
It has improved
now a little bit,

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:22.810
but again, we thought that
really doesn't get us out

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:25.180
of this capacity
planning thing, right?

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:27.430
We would still have to spin
up clusters and figure out

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:31.020
how many jobs, or which
jobs can be run on this one.

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:34.600
Whereas Google
Dataproc can help us

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:36.640
think in terms of
job scope clusters.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:39.520
So I'll get into the details
of that in a little bit.

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:41.820
And comparing
BigQuery and RedShift,

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:44.920
we definitely liked
BigQuery more, because, A,

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:45.960
it was truly serverless.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:50.260
It supported nested data
types, which we used a lot of.

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:53.590
And it was an added advantage
that it worked on Google Sheets

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:56.140
as a federated data
source, which would please

00:14:56.140 --> 00:14:58.070
a lot of people in the company.

00:14:58.070 --> 00:15:01.240
So it was definitely advantage
Google at this point.

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:03.640
And this is the
story that led us

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:08.670
to the decision point of where
we decided to take the plunge.

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:10.920
So in the next section, I
want to talk about how do we

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:12.870
get on from there, right?

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:16.000
Decisions like this
are seldom unanimous.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.530
There are counterpoints
within and outside the team.

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:22.220
And even for us
who were in favor,

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:24.720
we had to acknowledge
our failability here.

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:27.310
We had to acknowledge,
is this the right part?

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:30.720
Or if it is, how do I prove it?

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:33.510
So we started
thinking about, how

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:37.950
do we get some initial
success pointers?

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:40.810
How do we say that we
are on the right track?

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:43.200
So for that, what we
did is we partnered

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:45.870
with our analytics
team to identify

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:48.270
some of the important
datasets, and just

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:50.770
copied them over to BigQuery.

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:52.620
And then the Mode
visualization tool

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:56.710
that I talked about, we pointed
that to BigQuery instead.

00:15:56.710 --> 00:15:59.190
So there was some work
involved in changing the SQL

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:03.360
from the Impala SQL dialect
to BigQuery's standard SQL

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:03.860
dialect.

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:05.440
There were some minor changes.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:07.932
You had to handle the
timestamp fields differently,

00:16:07.932 --> 00:16:08.890
and so on and so forth.

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:10.590
So it took some kind
of documentation

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:12.390
and some education part of it.

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:15.640
But overall, not
such a daunting task.

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:19.330
And in order to keep
that data fresh,

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:22.690
what we did was we leveraged
our Airflow infrastructure

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:25.630
and inserted a
small cross-copy job

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:28.550
at the end of our data pipeline.

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:31.720
So we had this
bunch of processing

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:34.360
that would end up
producing a Parquet output.

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:35.920
And then we had
one downstream job

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.210
that would read
this Parquet file,

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:40.900
convert that into Airflow,
dump that into GCS,

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:43.510
an update that to BigQuery.

00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:45.520
So that data in
BigQuery remains fresh,

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:49.340
while still, all the
processing is on AWS.

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:52.699
So this gave us two benefits.

00:16:52.699 --> 00:16:54.240
In hindsight, I
think this turned out

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:57.960
to be a really good
call, A, because analysts

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:00.270
who were the number one
customers of this data

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:03.710
infrastructure gave a thumbs up.

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:05.990
I mean, their queries, they
found a quick turnaround

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:08.000
time from BigQuery.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.630
The failure and timeout rate
was markedly a lot lower

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:12.619
than before.

00:17:12.619 --> 00:17:14.930
So it was an immediate
win for them.

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:16.310
So that was good.

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:19.190
And this gave us a
good migration path.

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:21.200
So now we could
think about migration

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:24.349
without interrupting any
of the business workflows,

00:17:24.349 --> 00:17:26.869
interrupting any of
the other team's plans.

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:28.290
Your dashboards will still work.

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:29.880
The data is all still there.

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:33.680
While in the background,
one data pipeline at a time,

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:37.100
we can take and move
it from AWS to Google.

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:40.010
So with that headwind,
we really doubled down

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:42.890
and started building
some of the other pieces

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:45.560
on top of Google Cloud
that really enabled us

00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:48.740
to leverage this
infrastructure effectively.

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:53.480
And now I'll talk a little bit
more about job scoped clusters.

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.100
This is an entire
paradigm shift.

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:57.020
Now, instead of
thinking of setting up

00:17:57.020 --> 00:18:01.940
a cluster with this many
CPUs and this much memory,

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:05.550
and figuring out how many
jobs can run on this cluster,

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:08.750
now we could think about,
hey, I have this job.

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:10.910
What resources
does this job need?

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:13.640
Since a cluster can be spun
up in like 60 seconds, or 90

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:16.700
seconds, that job can
go ahead and spin up

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:20.420
the cluster it needs
and then just run,

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:22.820
without any interference
from any other job.

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:25.460
It just felt like
good parenting, right.

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:26.780
Job one you run in cluster one.

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:28.071
Job two you run in cluster two.

00:18:28.071 --> 00:18:29.200
No fighting.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:31.550
And it worked.

00:18:31.550 --> 00:18:34.430
And some of the other
things that we discovered

00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:36.800
while we were setting
up our info on Google

00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:41.360
was we realized that
for downstream jobs,

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:45.020
it was much more performant
and cost effective

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:47.540
to read it from GCS
rather than BigQuery.

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:50.690
So when you dump data into
BigQuery, you stage that in GCS

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:53.540
anyway, because that's
how the BQ load works.

00:18:53.540 --> 00:19:01.190
We just made sure that we gave
that part a formal convention,

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:04.460
so that we can address
our data on GCS as well.

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:09.350
And downstream Spark jobs
could just read data from GCS.

00:19:09.350 --> 00:19:14.790
We leveraged the audit logs
from both BigQuery and Dataproc,

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:17.510
fed it back into BigQuery so
that we can query it and clear

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:18.710
dashboards on it.

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:21.860
And we used that
to gain insights

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:26.270
into our usage and cost of how
we are using this platform.

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:30.860
And finally, we used the
fine-grained data access

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:35.660
and auth management in BigQuery
to really share data search

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:38.930
to a wide range of
teams, including

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:40.200
the contractors as well.

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:46.550
But the migration was not
without its challenges, right?

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:48.920
So the first two
I called out here

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:53.010
are required non-trivial
amount of code refactoring.

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:54.890
Now, we are dealing
with clusters

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:56.720
that we spin up and down.

00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:59.210
So HDFS is in
ephemeral storage now.

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:01.460
So we really had to move
all our data storage

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:06.200
into an object like
Google Cloud Storage.

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:08.780
At the time, BigQuery
did not support Parquet,

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:10.570
so we had to convert
everything into Avro.

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:15.154
And we got into some gnarly
data type conversion issues.

00:20:15.154 --> 00:20:17.750
But yeah, that
needed to be solved.

00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:20.870
A couple of runtime challenges--
one was a difference

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:22.910
in performance characteristics.

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:28.040
So Dataproc tries to fit two
Spark executors per worker.

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:29.720
And we realized that--

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:32.630
and it tries to allocate
roughly 50% of resources

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:34.490
to each of those executors.

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:37.340
And we realized that if we
tune a memory a little bit,

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.830
like heap memory or
out of heap memory,

00:20:39.830 --> 00:20:41.640
Dataproc would
sometimes only schedule

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:44.570
one executor on one worker.

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:47.210
And we would be at a loss of
we are throwing like this--

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:48.920
we have spun up this
24-node cluster,

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:50.570
but still it takes
twice as long.

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:51.590
I mean, why?

00:20:51.590 --> 00:20:54.260
And then we realized it's
only using half the cluster.

00:20:54.260 --> 00:21:00.740
So that took some Sherlock work
and investigation type thing.

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:03.260
And the other thing was
cross-cloud networking.

00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:05.182
Now we are moving
data back and forth,

00:21:05.182 --> 00:21:06.890
so we had to set up
some networking gear.

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:11.240
We had to set up the auth
keys that can be shared

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:13.790
and rotated in a good way.

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:15.890
And all these are from
a technical perspective.

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:18.350
And from a management and
organizational perspective,

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:21.110
what it meant is the
time spent on migration

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:24.020
is a time not spent
on the feature request

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:25.736
that all other teams have.

00:21:25.736 --> 00:21:27.860
So this is something that
we just asked the company

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:30.860
to take us up on good faith,
and were grateful for them

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:32.590
for their patience.

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:35.060
But we did overcome it.

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:38.450
And we did end up
having, in today's date,

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:42.200
we are leveraging
Google to a good effect.

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:45.205
We have compounded the
number of jobs and queries

00:21:45.205 --> 00:21:45.830
we are running.

00:21:45.830 --> 00:21:50.120
We create and destroy more
than 700 clusters per day.

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:52.850
We run more than
12,000 queries a day.

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:55.940
We added streaming
ETL capabilities.

00:21:55.940 --> 00:22:01.130
We used Deployment Manager to
have this entire configuration

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:03.110
on GCP as code.

00:22:03.110 --> 00:22:08.220
So all in all, I think we
did overcome all of that

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:10.310
to come to a very
good state here.

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:16.730
And we also used the Google's
organization and projects

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:19.970
hierarchy to create
multiple projects

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:22.700
and to control data visibility.

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:26.870
And also, to have a
per department billing,

00:22:26.870 --> 00:22:29.570
so to speak, so we
could figure out where

00:22:29.570 --> 00:22:31.160
our expenses are coming from.

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:37.060
Yeah, talk about this?

00:22:37.060 --> 00:22:38.260
Right.

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:41.290
So that's kind of
the story of how

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:44.440
we came to a decision point,
why we decided on GCP,

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:46.660
and how we migrated to GCP.

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:50.050
And in this last
section of this talk,

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:53.570
I want to just share the
thoughts as we reflect upon,

00:22:53.570 --> 00:22:55.710
so what do we make
of all that things?

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:58.600
How did this project go for us?

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:00.810
Starting with this
question that,

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:03.710
did we truly achieve what
we set out to achieve?

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:05.290
And several things come to mind.

00:23:05.290 --> 00:23:08.800
I mean, we currently
run, like I said,

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:10.300
a lot more queries,
a lot more jobs,

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:15.400
a lot more capabilities on
the data infrastructure.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:17.680
As well as we are
now, as a team,

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:23.170
taking on more problems, even
outside of the data scope.

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:25.360
So if I have to condense
all that into one word,

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:25.960
that's time.

00:23:25.960 --> 00:23:27.370
We gained time.

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:30.640
For a limited set of
engineers, we really

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:33.430
figured out that we could save
time and spend somewhere else

00:23:33.430 --> 00:23:35.060
wisely.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:37.610
And one other thing
that I think about

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:41.300
that we could not have
gained without this migration

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:44.360
is to truly democratize data.

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:48.500
The scale of BigQuery, the
scale and the flexibility

00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:52.400
with creating both normal
and materialized views

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:56.120
and have different auth
scopes for each one of them

00:23:56.120 --> 00:24:01.130
has really helped us to shared
datasets with almost all

00:24:01.130 --> 00:24:06.110
of the organizations, all the
departments within the company.

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:10.260
And everybody across the
company is leveraging that data.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:13.640
So that's a huge win for us.

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:16.560
So those are the
good aspects of it.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:20.130
So there is also one more way
to think about all these things

00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:22.510
as there are plenty of
risks along the way.

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:24.810
So how do we think we
did with the risks?

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:28.430
Starting with planning fallacy.

00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:31.590
So planning fallacy is
when you are making a plan,

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:32.880
you're super optimistic.

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.710
I'll do this in
like three months.

00:24:35.710 --> 00:24:38.089
But you overshoot it, right?

00:24:38.089 --> 00:24:39.130
And we did fell for this.

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:42.430
We started like we
should be able to do

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:45.820
this within six months, from
MVP to all the way to the end.

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:50.500
But we did bleed into the
third quarter of the migration.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:53.470
But we did put our heads
down and complete it.

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:58.270
The other estimate that
we overshot by a lot

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:01.030
is we thought we would
spend less on Google.

00:25:01.030 --> 00:25:03.740
But that did not
turn out to be true.

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:06.430
But that's OK, because
now we are actually

00:25:06.430 --> 00:25:09.880
spending more because we are
using that data a lot more.

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:11.570
We are running a lot more jobs.

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:12.986
We are running a
lot more queries.

00:25:12.986 --> 00:25:15.280
And that's where
the cost comes from.

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:20.050
The second risk I think about
is the sunk cost fallacy.

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:25.120
Sometimes these migrations keep
going on for months and months.

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:28.370
And somewhere along the halfway
through, you think about, hey,

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:29.770
I've come this far.

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:31.720
And that becomes the
only reason for just kind

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:33.400
of keep going forward.

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:36.490
It happens when you don't
have an option to go back,

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:41.440
or going back becomes
really expensive, right?

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:46.794
And this is an item where
I think we did really well.

00:25:46.794 --> 00:25:50.770
A, because we always had
a path of going back.

00:25:50.770 --> 00:25:53.170
We didn't dismantle
our AWS infrastructure

00:25:53.170 --> 00:25:55.610
until we were completely here.

00:25:55.610 --> 00:25:58.460
So if we had hit a roadblock,
if we, at any point of time,

00:25:58.460 --> 00:26:01.960
thought that, hey, this
is not working out,

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:03.640
we had other alternatives.

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:06.160
And one other thing
that helped us is we

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:08.560
had scoped in the
beginning that we are only

00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:10.210
going to move our
data infrastructure

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:11.770
and nothing more.

00:26:11.770 --> 00:26:13.960
And that scope
remained unaltered

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:15.730
throughout this project.

00:26:15.730 --> 00:26:19.940
And that was, again, one
other good thing in hindsight.

00:26:19.940 --> 00:26:21.770
So these two are
more with respect

00:26:21.770 --> 00:26:24.840
to the migration,
migration related risks.

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:27.270
The third risk I
want to talk about

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:29.810
is more of an existential
risk when you operate

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:31.570
in a multi-cloud environment.

00:26:31.570 --> 00:26:34.920
So Conway's law, I don't know
if you've heard about it.

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:38.900
It states that organizations
will produce designs

00:26:38.900 --> 00:26:41.510
that will have the communication
structures that they

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:43.250
have within the organization.

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:47.360
What it translates to in
reality is if your one team is

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:50.060
responsible for your
Google infrastructure,

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:53.780
if the some second team is
a responsible for your AWS

00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:56.990
infrastructure, when you
think about solutions,

00:26:56.990 --> 00:27:02.990
can you truly think about one
single base data center infra?

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:05.240
Or otherwise, I mean,
if the teams work well,

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:05.930
you can do that.

00:27:05.930 --> 00:27:07.700
If the teams don't
work well together,

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:09.950
you would have one solution
here and one solution here

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:12.470
that would not talk
to each other, right?

00:27:12.470 --> 00:27:15.810
So like I said, this
is an existential risk.

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:17.970
You're completely not
out of the woods yet.

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:20.330
But the good thing
is just being aware

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:23.300
of it is a very good step.

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:26.010
And this is something that we
have to be mindful of from here

00:27:26.010 --> 00:27:26.510
on.

00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:27.500
This never ends.

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:34.460
So that's was a brief
scorecard on how we did.

00:27:34.460 --> 00:27:36.560
So now I'll take
a liberty to give

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:41.070
a scorecard on how Google did.

00:27:41.070 --> 00:27:45.330
Overall, I think the uptime
has been pretty solid.

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:50.810
The scale-- that scale and load
that we threw at Google, Google

00:27:50.810 --> 00:27:53.390
has handled, no big deal.

00:27:57.260 --> 00:28:00.800
Their support teams have
been pretty responsive,

00:28:00.800 --> 00:28:04.460
for both low priority issues as
well as high priority issues.

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:05.940
These were the good parts.

00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:09.057
But that does not mean that
there were no issues at all.

00:28:09.057 --> 00:28:10.390
They're also a software company.

00:28:10.390 --> 00:28:11.515
They also rely on software.

00:28:11.515 --> 00:28:13.550
Things go wrong.

00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:17.970
Some of the things I wanted to
highlight was, like last year,

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:22.724
we had a couple of
BigQuery related incidents.

00:28:22.724 --> 00:28:24.390
Essentially, they
were breaking changes.

00:28:24.390 --> 00:28:26.098
They were not backward
compatible changes

00:28:26.098 --> 00:28:31.040
at Google's end that kind of
brought our data processing

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:33.070
to a grinding halt.

00:28:33.070 --> 00:28:38.690
One of these was, for example,
the nullability of Avro fields

00:28:38.690 --> 00:28:41.150
and nullability of
BigQuery fields.

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:44.570
The requirements were made
strict that they should match,

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:46.460
which wasn't the case before.

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:48.530
And we were freely
using strict fields

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:50.870
and mapping them to
nullable fields in BigQuery,

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:53.160
so that broke everything.

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:56.090
The other thing was, again, some
charting changes that we had

00:28:56.090 --> 00:28:59.360
to set a particular variable.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:03.590
And more recently, we had a
bunch of issues with Dataproc.

00:29:03.590 --> 00:29:07.890
There was one where the GC
images weren't available.

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:10.350
There were a couple in June.

00:29:10.350 --> 00:29:12.030
Again, one related
to some networking

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:15.240
and one of something
else in the backend.

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:18.060
And both of these were,
unfortunately, kind

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:20.440
of impacted the entire region.

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:22.857
So maybe that's one CBB.

00:29:22.857 --> 00:29:24.440
That's a term that
we use in Thumbtack

00:29:24.440 --> 00:29:26.170
called Could Be Better.

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:29.130
So one Could Be Better
that I would call out

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:33.240
is probably have a rollout
plan that, even if it goes bad,

00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:36.390
does not impact
the entire region.

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:39.060
Maybe just a few
[INAUDIBLE] within a region.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:42.630
But it did force us
to rethink, and we

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:44.520
were able to
leverage our Airflow

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:46.560
infrastructure to move
from a single region

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:47.940
to be multi-region.

00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:50.460
And since the BigQuery
and the GCS layer

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:53.490
were multi-region anyway,
it was easy for us to do so.

00:29:56.590 --> 00:30:00.970
And another Could Be Better
that I would call out is,

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:05.200
for example, that April issue
had an amazing postmortem,

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:06.430
but the June issue didn't.

00:30:06.430 --> 00:30:08.680
So it's all a great
learning experience

00:30:08.680 --> 00:30:11.680
to learn about what went wrong.

00:30:11.680 --> 00:30:14.590
We can understand more about
how everything works internally.

00:30:14.590 --> 00:30:17.110
And sometimes it can
get to action items

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:19.700
for us, that if
we could do this,

00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:23.920
we could protect ourselves
a little bit more.

00:30:23.920 --> 00:30:27.060
But overall, I think
it's a great time

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:29.455
to be out there and building
products on these cloud

00:30:29.455 --> 00:30:31.080
platforms, that you
don't have to think

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:35.670
about so many complex and
sophisticated infrastructure

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:36.450
things.

00:30:36.450 --> 00:30:39.210
Google, given its
scale with Search,

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:41.550
has solved these problems.

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:43.890
Their infrastructure works
from mind-boggling scale

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:45.720
and complexity.

00:30:45.720 --> 00:30:49.920
And it's great that
they are making

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:52.411
it available for
other people to use it

00:30:52.411 --> 00:30:53.660
to their cloud infrastructure.

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:55.890
I mean, which other
company in this world

00:30:55.890 --> 00:30:59.790
can think of building
a spanner, for example?

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:03.340
But as users of it,
it still takes work.

00:31:03.340 --> 00:31:06.070
It still takes a lot
of thought on how

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:07.470
do you want to go about it.

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:09.090
It still takes dedication.

00:31:09.090 --> 00:31:11.490
It still takes coming
up with a cohesive plan

00:31:11.490 --> 00:31:13.350
and making tradeoffs.

00:31:13.350 --> 00:31:16.090
So with that, I'll
conclude my talk.

00:31:16.090 --> 00:31:17.710
Thank you very
much for listening.

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:19.668
And if you have any
questions, I can take that.

00:31:19.668 --> 00:31:22.170
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:24.049 --> 00:31:25.590
RYAN LIPPERT: So
Venky, thanks a lot.

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:26.964
That was a terrific
presentation.

00:31:26.964 --> 00:31:28.750
I think that everyone
here has enjoyed it.

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:30.166
Because I've got
this special mic,

00:31:30.166 --> 00:31:32.920
I'll kick off with a few
questions just to start.

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:34.420
One of the first
things I thought

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:35.740
the crowd might be
interested in hearing about

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:38.060
is, you talked a lot
about multi-cloud there.

00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:40.030
Can you talk a little
bit about the egress cost

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:42.480
there, and what that's
been like for Thumbtack?

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:43.230
VENKY IYER: Right.

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:47.830
So we're kind of exporting
a lot of data out of AWS,

00:31:47.830 --> 00:31:51.280
so we're mainly talking
about egress costs in AWS.

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:56.120
It's not been that high, in
the sense that, for example,

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:58.780
our Sqoop jobs still run.

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:01.870
Every single time they run,
they take the entire database

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:03.430
snapshot.

00:32:03.430 --> 00:32:05.540
There are some kind of
nice to have optimizations.

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:06.998
We could have
streaming replication

00:32:06.998 --> 00:32:10.030
into Google, which would
reduce the egress costs.

00:32:10.030 --> 00:32:13.570
But overall, those
figures are not that high

00:32:13.570 --> 00:32:16.690
that we have to drop everything
and fix that thing right now.

00:32:16.690 --> 00:32:19.000
Like I said, again,
this goes in terms

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:20.860
of how we think about our time.

00:32:20.860 --> 00:32:23.500
We prioritize
products and projects

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:26.020
that are important to our
company's top line first.

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:28.750
But all in all, I
think that egress costs

00:32:28.750 --> 00:32:31.030
are pretty much under
control and manageable.

00:32:31.030 --> 00:32:31.990
RYAN LIPPERT:
That's great, Venky.

00:32:31.990 --> 00:32:33.448
And so I think
another thing you've

00:32:33.448 --> 00:32:36.250
talked a lot about where you've
been and where you've come to.

00:32:36.250 --> 00:32:37.960
What's next for
you at Thumbtack?

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:40.390
What are you thinking about
in terms of the new use cases

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:42.610
you're going after, or what's
next for your infrastructure

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:44.680
there, as you think about data
and analytics in particular?

00:32:44.680 --> 00:32:45.490
VENKY IYER: Right.

00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:48.490
So having the robust
infrastructure

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:50.944
like this for base
data processing really

00:32:50.944 --> 00:32:52.360
becomes the
foundation on which we

00:32:52.360 --> 00:32:54.940
can build some more
sophisticated and complex

00:32:54.940 --> 00:32:55.540
processing.

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:57.520
We are now trying
to invest a lot

00:32:57.520 --> 00:33:00.180
into our machine
learning infrastructure

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:01.475
using Tensorflow.

00:33:01.475 --> 00:33:05.470
Currently we are using
Tensorflow serving on AWS,

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:09.220
but we would like to
evaluate Cloud ML.

00:33:09.220 --> 00:33:15.310
As well as we are evaluating
Dataflow to create the feature

00:33:15.310 --> 00:33:18.040
extraction pipelines that we
could run in both streaming

00:33:18.040 --> 00:33:19.210
and batch modes.

00:33:19.210 --> 00:33:21.310
So those are some of
the things that are

00:33:21.310 --> 00:33:23.110
coming up and in the pipeline.

00:33:23.110 --> 00:33:23.680
RYAN LIPPERT: That sounds great.

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:26.230
And if I might, just one more
question for you before let's

00:33:26.230 --> 00:33:28.030
open it up to the audience.

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:30.940
When you think about what we can
do from a Google perspective,

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:33.270
or what other services that
we could potentially offer,

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:35.115
is there anything in
our stack that you'd

00:33:35.115 --> 00:33:37.240
like to see that maybe
you're not currently seeing,

00:33:37.240 --> 00:33:38.810
that we could be working on?

00:33:38.810 --> 00:33:40.870
VENKY IYER: Yeah, so I
think one of the things

00:33:40.870 --> 00:33:42.610
that I missed on
Google infrastructure

00:33:42.610 --> 00:33:46.930
while setting up everything was
having a pub/sub system that

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:49.510
supported some level
of partial ordering.

00:33:49.510 --> 00:33:52.880
I'm a huge fan of the Kafka
paradigm of messaging, Kafka

00:33:52.880 --> 00:33:54.170
or Apache Pulsar.

00:33:54.170 --> 00:33:56.830
Both have some partial ordering,
so that enables, for example,

00:33:56.830 --> 00:34:02.230
you to have database streams
into your pub/sub system.

00:34:02.230 --> 00:34:05.040
Google Pub/Sub has taken
a different philosophy

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:06.050
for scalability.

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:08.000
They're updated off
the ordering part.

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:11.070
That's because the Google
Dataflow's stream processing

00:34:11.070 --> 00:34:12.940
pipeline is very feature rich.

00:34:12.940 --> 00:34:16.690
It can do some even time
windowing and reorder there.

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:18.969
But if you are not
really using that,

00:34:18.969 --> 00:34:22.150
we would really want to
see some heavy lifting done

00:34:22.150 --> 00:34:23.469
on the pub/sub side.

00:34:23.469 --> 00:34:26.320
I think that would be
very useful for us.

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:29.170
And I've recently
learned that, I think,

00:34:29.170 --> 00:34:30.880
Confluent and Google
are partnering

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:32.989
to set up managed Kafka on GCP.

00:34:32.989 --> 00:34:36.520
So that's something we would
check it out and see what

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:37.900
we can build on top of that.

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:40.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

